# Minima Rules

- Minima RPC commands should be sent as a single URL path command, not as query parameters. Build the command string first, for example `megammrsync action:resync host:megammr.minima.global:9001`, then percent-encode it into the path: `http://minima:9005/megammrsync%20action%3Aresync%20host%3Amegammr.minima.global%3A9001`.
- Do not expose a generic Minima command proxy. Add narrow, allowlisted backend actions for each supported command.
- The RPC console (`backend/src/features/minima/minima-console.catalog.ts` / `minima-console.service.ts`) is a deliberate, scoped exception to the rule above, not a generic proxy: it only ever runs a command that is both present in the static, closed-world catalog and enabled in the admin whitelist (`GET`/`POST /api/minima/console/whitelist`), so no request body can execute anything outside that catalog. `vault`, `sendfrom`, `signfrom`, `createfrom`, `postfrom`, `createtokenfrom`, `decryptbackup`, `keys`, and `quit` are hard-excluded — not offered as checkboxes at all — because they can expose or accept raw wallet private keys/seed phrases, or (for `quit`) halt the node with no UI recovery path. Every other catalog command defaults to enabled if read-only and disabled if it mutates anything (funds, chain, config, network, wallet). Whitelist edits require re-entering the admin PIN/password (`POST /api/minima/console/whitelist`, same re-auth pattern as `changePassword`). Where a whitelisted command already has a dedicated narrow action (`megammrsync` → `resyncMegammr()`, `peers action:addpeers` → `addMinimaPeers()`, `backup` → `createBackup()`, `restoresync` → `restoreBackup()`), the console dispatches to that same function instead of re-implementing the RPC call, so operation-tracking, audit logging, and error normalization stay a single source of truth.
- Node backup/restore (`backend/src/features/minima/minima-backup.service.ts`, `POST/GET/DELETE /api/minima/backups*`) is a first-class, UI-driven feature, not just console passthrough: `backup` writes a `.bak` file (seed phrase, private keys, coin proofs, transaction history — a superset of the seed-phrase-only wallet import in `wallet.service.ts`) into the shared `${MINIMA_DATA_DIR}/backups` host directory, and restore always uses `restoresync` (restore + Megammr re-sync in one step) against the configured Megammr host. Plain `restore` (no resync) and `reset` stay console-only, raw passthrough. Downloading or restoring a backup requires re-entering the admin PIN/password, same re-auth pattern as the console whitelist, because a backup file contains key material protected only by the one stored backup password.
- `${MINIMA_DATA_DIR}/backups` is mounted into **both** containers: into `backend` read-write at the fixed path `/minima-backups` (the only backend↔minima shared host directory — never the rest of `MINIMA_DATA_DIR`), and into `minima` at `/home/minima/backups`. The second mount is required, not cosmetic: verified live against the pinned image, Minima resolves the `backup`/`restoresync` `file:` argument relative to its home dir (`/home/minima`), not `/home/minima/data` — without this mount, `backup file:backups/<name>.bak` silently lands in the container's ephemeral layer and is lost on restart, invisible to `backend`.
- One admin-chosen backup password (`GET`/`POST`/`DELETE /api/minima/backups/password`, re-auth required for both setting/changing and removing) is stored encrypted (`shared/crypto.ts`'s `encryptSecret`, keyed by `APP_SECRET` — same primitive as Integritas token storage) and used for every backup, manual or automatic — there is no unencrypted/weakly-protected class of backup. Removing the password (`clearBackupPassword()`) also turns off automatic backups, since there is no longer a password for the scheduler to use. All backups (manual and automatic alike) share one rolling list capped at 20 (`MAX_BACKUPS` in `minima-backup.service.ts`), oldest auto-deleted once a new one pushes past the cap; filenames still encode trigger source (`minima-manual-<ts>.bak` / `minima-auto-<ts>.bak`) for operator visibility even though the UI shows one combined list. All of them are downloadable and restorable.
- Automatic backups are created by the backend's own scheduler (`minima-backup-scheduler.service.ts`, started from `index.ts`), not Minima's built-in `backup auto:true`. Verified against Minima's own source (`minima-global/Minima`): `backup` always AES-encrypts, but falls back to the hardcoded public default password `"minima"` when none is supplied, and Minima's built-in daily scheduler always calls a bare, hardcoded `backup` command (`Main.java`) — no configurable password or `file:`, ever — so it can never deliver a real password or a location the backend can see. Our own scheduler avoids both problems by calling `createBackup({ auto: true })` directly with the stored password into the shared folder. It runs at a fixed nightly clock time (00:30) rather than a rolling 24h interval from container start, so it lands overnight instead of at whatever hour the container happened to boot; this uses the backend container's own system clock, so set the `TZ` env var (docker-compose.yml, default `UTC`) to the Pi's real local timezone for "nightly" to mean actual local night.
- Automatic node restarts (`GET`/`POST /api/minima/restart/auto`, opt-in, off by default) reuse that same nightly scheduler instead of a second timer: each nightly tick runs the backup first, then — only if auto-restart is enabled and at least 48 hours have passed since the last one (tracked via a persisted setting, not in-memory, so a backend redeploy doesn't reset the cadence or fire early) — calls the same `restartMinimaContainer()` used by the manual restart button. This is a preventive node-health measure (per internal recommendation), not a response to any detected fault, so it runs unconditionally on that cadence rather than being gated on stall detection. **Currently disabled**: the nightly tick's call into `runAutoRestartIfDue()` is commented out in `minima-backup-scheduler.service.ts`, and the settings-panel toggle is commented out in the frontend, until automations get graceful handling around node restarts (a restart mid-run has no coordination with an in-progress automation today). The setting/routes/service functions are left intact for an easy re-enable.
- `POST /api/minima/restart` performs a graceful restart, not a bare `docker restart`: it sends `quit compact:true` over RPC first (`quit` still has no console catalog entry at all per the exclusion above — this is a narrow, wrapped internal use, never a raw whitelist-able command). Verified against a real node: `quit` can report `{"status":true,"message":"Shutdown complete"}` well before the process actually exits (from under a second up to several seconds, sometimes longer), and Docker's own `unless-stopped` policy — not our code — is usually what relaunches the container once it does, often before an RPC-response-based check could ever observe it. So detection compares the container's Docker-reported `RestartCount`/`StartedAt` against a baseline captured before sending `quit` (`getContainerRestartBaseline`/`waitForContainerRestart` in `docker.control.ts`), not a transient "not running" state, and waits up to 5 minutes before giving up — short timeouts here are actively counterproductive, since forcing a SIGTERM/SIGKILL while the node is still legitimately mid-shutdown/compacting is the same wallet/chain DB corruption risk `quit compact:true` exists to avoid. Only past that window does it fall back to the previous forceful restart (`restartComposeService()`, Docker `/restart` — SIGTERM then SIGKILL after `t=10`), so the action still guarantees a restart either way. The quit/wait/start sequence runs in the background — the route still responds immediately with `{ state: "restarting" }`, same as before, and the existing operation-tracking (`beginMinimaOperation`/`isMinimaOperationInProgress`, its max window raised to 6 minutes to cover this) keeps the UI showing "restarting" until the regular status poller sees the node running again.
